<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conquest of Wales by Edward I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1277">1277</a>–<a href="/years/1283">1283</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> by Edward I was the series of English campaigns of 1277 and 1282–1283 that ended the independent Welsh principality ruled from Gwynedd. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> was killed in December 1282 and his brother Dafydd captured, Edward annexed the principality, imposed English administration through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan">Statute of Rhuddlan</a> (1284) and built castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, Conwy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conquest-of-wales-by-edward-i.jpg" alt="Conquest of Wales by Edward I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_I_-_Westminster_Abbey_Sedilia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After 1066 the Normans seized much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and set up Marcher lordships, but Welsh principalities such as Gwynedd, Powys and Deheubarth survived, and by the 13th century Gwynedd was dominant, its princes styling themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> of 1267, Henry III recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> in that title in return for homage. Under Edward I tension grew: in 1274 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-gwenwynwyn">Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn</a> of Powys and Llywelyn's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dafydd-ap-gruffydd">Dafydd ap Gruffydd</a> defected to the English, Llywelyn refused to do homage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> in 1275 and planned to marry Eleanor, daughter of Simon de Montfort, and in November 1276 Edward declared war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 1277 Edward marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> into Gwynedd with 15,500 men, 9,000 of them Welshmen from the south, supported by a fleet from the Cinque Ports. Llywelyn surrendered without a major battle, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aberconwy">Treaty of Aberconwy</a> in November 1277 he kept only western Gwynedd and his title.</p><p>In 1282 Dafydd, discontented with his reward, rebelled, and the war became a national struggle provoked largely by Edward's attempts to impose English law. The Welsh won at Llandeilo Fawr in June 1282 and at Moel-y-don on 6 November, but Llywelyn was lured into a trap and killed at the Battle of Orewin Bridge on 11 December. Edward marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> in January 1283 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolwyddelan-castle">Dolwyddelan Castle</a>; Dafydd was captured in June and executed as a traitor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Lands not kept by the crown became new Marcher lordships, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a> for the Earl of Lincoln. In 1301 the royal lands were granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward of Caernarfon</a> with the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, thereafter the customary endowment of the heir to the throne. English settlers took Welsh land and new towns such as Flint, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a> were founded, while James of Saint George oversaw the building of castles such as Beaumaris.</p><p>Revolts followed, under Madog ap Llywelyn in 1294, Llywelyn Bren in 1316 to 1318 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> from 1400, but none succeeded, and the Laws in Wales Acts 1535 to 1542 incorporated Wales into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The conquest cost Edward around £173,000, against an annual revenue of about £40,000, and his need for taxes helped widen the role of Parliament.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conquest of Wales by Edward I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
